WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.600
A principal is an identity that can request access to a cloud resource or API.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:10.260
Common building blocks include users, groups, roles, service accounts, managed identities,

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:16.880
workload identities, applications, API keys, access tokens, and temporary credentials.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.180
Different providers use different names.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:25.000
AWS roles, Azure managed identities, and Google Cloud service accounts are not identical,

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.380
but they all represent a basic idea:

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.600
something is being authenticated and then authorized to perform actions.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:35.340
Human identities and non-human identities need different controls.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:40.040
A person may sign in through a browser or command-line tool using a federated identity

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:41.140
provider,

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.560
multi-factor authentication, and role assumption.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.500
A workload may run inside a virtual machine, container, serverless function,

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:53.800
Kubernetes pod, CI/CD pipeline, or deployment automation.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:59.460
It may need to read a secret, write logs, publish an artifact, deploy infrastructure,

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.520
or call another cloud service.

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.260
The security design should reflect the identity type and job.

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:10.200
Every identity should have a purpose, owner, lifecycle, and permission boundary.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.500
Purpose explains why it exists.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.840
Ownership explains who reviews it, approves changes,

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.620
and responds when something looks wrong.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:23.800
Lifecycle explains when it should be created, rotated, disabled, or deleted.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:28.020
A permission boundary or equivalent guardrail limits what the identity can do,

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.040
even if a policy or role assignment changes later.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.620
These details prevent stale identities from becoming quiet risk.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:36.440
Credentials also differ.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:41.220
Long-lived access keys and static secrets are operationally risky because they can be

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:42.320
copied,

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.300
forgotten, or leaked.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.560
Temporary credentials, managed identity mechanisms, workload identity federation,

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:53.060
and short-lived tokens reduce that risk when implemented correctly.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.760
The goal is not merely to create identities.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:02:01.340
The goal is to make each identity understandable, owned, scoped, auditable,

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:04.200
and removable when its purpose ends.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:07.460
Human identities usually follow people and job roles.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:13.200
They need onboarding, role changes, offboarding, MFA, and periodic review.

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:16.460
Non-human identities follow systems and automation.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:21.720
They need owners, rotation or short-lived credentials, clear deployment context,

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:24.500
and removal when the workload retires.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.520
Treating both categories the same leads to mistakes: humans get permanent machine keys,

00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:33.180
or workloads inherit human-style administrator privileges.

00:02:33.400 --> 00:02:35.240
Naming and metadata help.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:39.380
An identity name, tag, label, description,

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:42.640
or owner field should tell a reviewer what the identity is for.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:47.580
If a service account called temp-admin has existed for two years and no one knows what it

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:48.760
runs,

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.780
that is not only untidy; it is a risk.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:57.360
Identity inventory is the foundation for access review, incident response, and cleanup.

00:02:57.580 --> 00:03:01.340
Reviews should ask whether an identity still matches reality.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.480
Does the group still represent the team?

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:05.860
Does the role still match the application?

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:07.760
Is the API key still needed?

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.820
Is the token issuer still trusted?

00:03:10.040 --> 00:03:14.440
IAM drift often comes from identities that were correct when created but were never

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:16.580
revisited after projects,

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.120
teams, and workloads changed.
